"""Content D: Ch7 Liminal + Ogham/Symbols (pp119-132), Ch8 Charms (pp133-142),
Ch9 Ancestors (pp143-147), Ch10 Oath (pp148-150)."""

CH7 = [
("lore", "Liminal Places", "Wells that remember",
 ["Picture the mud of a lakebed holding bronze swords and cauldrons and scattered coins, laid down by hands across thousands of years. Divers and diggers keep finding them where rivers slow and springs rise, and scholars call these placed gifts votive deposits. The custom never fully died. On pattern days at holy wells you can still watch people walk the round, pray their stations in order, and knot rags to the thorn above the water, while the water itself holds this memory longer than any record book. Some gifts went in whole and some bent or broken, as if the giving mattered more than the keeping.",
  "Read the well as a neighbor with a long memory, not a machine that grants wishes. The old deposits are attested, lifted from the ground and catalogued shelf by shelf. The rounds and the rag trees are living folk custom, kept by the families who tend each site and know its days. Tossing coins into a living spring is a newer visitor habit, and the metal fouls the water it pretends to honor, so leave that part out. When in doubt, watch what the keepers do and follow their lead before your own impulse.",
  "Your nearest spring or city well can be visited in under an hour with nothing in your pockets. Walk quietly, follow the round as it is kept there, or circle once sunwise where no round is marked. Tie only cloth that will rot away on its own, ask leave before you lift any water, and sit a while at the edge to listen before you leave. Where the well is built over or capped, stand at the nearest marked place and pay your attention there instead.",
  "<b>Lore Root:</b> votive deposits plus living well-custom — Attested and Folk survival."],
 "Which spring or well is nearest you, and on what day will you go and see it?"),
("lore", "Liminal Places", "Fords where laws hold",
 ["The river runs shallow over gravel, and an army must cross single file. In the great old Irish tale called the Tain, the young hero Cu Chulainn holds one such ford alone against the armies of Connacht, meeting each challenger man to man while the rest wait their turn at the banks. A ford belongs to neither side, and the storytellers loved that narrow ground, setting their hardest bargains and single combats where the water runs thin. The armies camp on either bank and watch, because the ford makes a courtroom of the river.",
  "Midstream has its own rule: neither bank owns the middle, so everyone must state terms in the open and keep them. Your own fords work the same way: the half-left job, the half-bought house, the sentence half said. Name what you are leaving and what you are carrying while you stand midstream, instead of sleepwalking to the far side. Speak your terms out loud while the water is still around your ankles.",
  "Take twenty minutes at your table with paper and pen. Write the crossing you are inside right now, the bank behind you, and the bank ahead. List one thing you will carry over and one you will set down in the water, read the page aloud once, and keep it folded where you dress each morning until the crossing ends. Burn nothing and bury nothing; the paper is only a marker, and the speaking is the rite.",
  "<b>Lore Root:</b> ford as boundary-battle place, Tain pattern — Attested literary pattern."],
 "Describe the crossing you are in the middle of now, and name the bank you are leaving behind."),
("lore", "Liminal Places", "Doorways you own",
 ["Every threshold you step through is a small ford between out and in. Folk custom has always treated it that way: rowan sprigs nailed above the lintel, a pinch of salt along the sill, a verse sung going out and a pause for breath coming home. You cross that line a dozen times a day without noticing, and the old habit asks you to notice just once, with your hand on the frame. Stand in your own frame tonight and feel how the air changes on either side of the line.",
  "That doorway marks the edge of your household the way a bank marks a river, and small care there steadies everything inside. The rowan and the salt and the verses are folk survivals, gathered from many districts rather than one single rite. A modern monthly keeping, clean and dress and speak, gives those scattered scraps a shape your calendar can hold. The door you keep becomes the standard for the rest: what holds at the threshold spreads inward.",
  "Pick the door you use most and give it fifteen minutes this week. Scrub the sill, clear the corners, hang a sprig of green that suits your street. Choose one short line for leaving and one for returning, and say them daily for a month. Small repetition does what grandeur cannot. Photograph the dressed doorway for your journal, then leave the keeping to hands and voice rather than the camera.",
  "<b>Lore Root:</b> threshold-keeping across folk custom — Folk survival."],
 "Which doorway will you keep, and what two short lines will it hear from you?"),
("lore", "Liminal Places", "Dawn, dusk, and Samhain Eve",
 ["Twice a day the light stands neither here nor there, and once a year the season does the same. At Samhain Eve, summer's end, the old Irish tales say the mounds, the grass-covered burial hills, stood open and the dead walked near. Dawn and dusk carry the smaller daily version of that opening, when the house is quiet and the edges of things show themselves plainly. Stand out in it once and feel the day change hands, with birds passing the watch from one shift to the other. Even a city window will do.",
  "Edges were treated as meeting times, when bargains get struck and messages pass, and the tales return to that pattern again and again. That pattern is attested across story after story. The phrase thin places for such spots is modern, a recent coinage worth labeling, but the habit underneath is older: pay attention at the seams of the day and something in you answers. No doctrine is asked of you here, only punctuality: show up at the edge and pay attention.",
  "Keep three dawns or three dusks in a row, ten minutes each, at a window or a step. Watch the light change and count three sounds before you go back inside. Write a dated line or two after each sit, and by the third day you will know whether dawn or dusk is yours. Where cloud hides the sun, keep the hour anyway; the edge holds even when you cannot see it.",
  "<b>Lore Root:</b> edge-meetings in tale and custom — Attested pattern; thin-place term — Modern."],
 "Will you sit at dawn or at dusk, and which three days will you keep?"),
("journal", "Liminal Places", "Well visit: go, clean, listen. Date, place, what you cleared, what you heard, what you left.", 12),
("journal", "Liminal Places", "Ford worksheet: what you are crossing, what you carry over, what you set down mid-river.", 12),
("lore", "Liminal Places", "The doorway rite",
 ["Set aside four minutes at the door you use most, once a month or at each fire festival. Sweep the sill clean down to the bare step and lay a fresh line of salt where the step meets the street. Set rowan above the lintel where rowan grows, rosemary where it does not. Stand inside a breath, then speak or sing one verse as you step out into the day. Move briskly and speak plainly; the rite wants care, not ceremony.",
  "Each part carries its household sense, drawn from folk custom rather than one old book. The sweeping clears stale passage, the salt marks the line, the green over the lintel signs the house awake. The verse going out and the thanks coming home frame the day the way banks frame a ford, and the monthly shape holding it all is modern, honestly labeled so. Nothing is burned, nothing is buried, and nothing leaves the house but stale dust. The broom does half the blessing before a word is said.",
  "Coming home, halt in the frame itself for one breath and one thanks, and mark what the light is doing as you stand there. Four minutes is the whole of it, going and coming both. Write your two lines down tonight so they stand ready when the month turns. Miss a month and you have lost nothing; sweep, salt, green, and verse begin again without penalty. Four minutes, once a month: even the busiest house can hold that.",
  "<b>Lore Root:</b> threshold elements across folk custom — Folk survival; monthly shape — Modern."],
 "Write out your short going out verse and your coming home thanks, so both are ready to keep."),
("journal", "Liminal Places", "Three-day sit log: dawn OR dusk, three days. Light, sounds, what shifted — dated.", 12),
("lore", "Ogham", "Stones with strokes",
 ["Run your finger along the edge of an Irish standing stone and you may find short strokes cut in bundles along a center line. That is ogham, an early medieval Irish alphabet whose stones stand roughly from the fourth through sixth centuries, most of them naming a man as somebody's son in plain words. Later scribes copied the alphabet into manuscripts and kept it alive on vellum after the carving stopped. You can see them still in Irish fields and museums, weathered but legible where the light rakes sideways.",
  "Centuries later, medieval poets joined each letter to a tree with a riddle verse, the kennings: birch for Beith, rowan for Luis, on through the grove. Those tree lists are attested in the manuscripts, yet they come from poets rather than from the stones themselves. Casting sticks to tell fortunes is a modern game laid over the old alphabet, pleasant at the table but always labeled modern. Learn the difference by handling: the stones give names, the poets give trees, and your own sticks give counsel.",
  "Copy one letter slowly in your journal with a ruler for the stem line. Count its strokes, sound its name aloud, and note what the shape brings to mind before any meaning. One letter a week through the month teaches your hand what no list can. Date each copy and say the sound aloud; the alphabet enters through hand and mouth together.",
  "<b>Lore Root:</b> stones 4th-6th c. plus medieval tree lists — Attested; oracle use — Modern."],
 "Copy one ogham letter slowly here. What do its strokes bring to mind before any meaning?"),
("lore", "Ogham", "Seven fews for divination",
 ["Seven short strokes of the old alphabet are all this book asks you to learn, and the tradition calls each letter a few. Birch for beginnings, rowan for protection, alder for standing firm where the ground is soft. Willow for water and moon and grief, ash for the links between worlds, hawthorn for the hindrance that guards, holly for outlasting the hard season. Say each name aloud until the sounds sit easy on your tongue. Copy the seven onto slips of card tonight, the name on one side and the meaning on the other.",
  "Those letter names and tree joins come from the medieval lists, honestly attested there. Everything else in this working, the narrowed set of seven and the three-few spread of root and trunk and branch, is modern, built for journal reflection rather than old divination. Call it what it is and it stays clean: a modern mirror in an old wooden frame. Nothing here pretends to old divination, and that honesty is what keeps the working trustworthy.",
  "Boil your question down to one written line tonight, then draw three fews from slips of paper and lay them left to right. The root shows what grounds the matter, the trunk what stands firm, the branch what may grow. Date the cast, write three plain sentences, and read it back at the next fire before you draw again. Keep every cast in the same pages, and patterns will surface across the months that one night cannot show.",
  "<b>Lore Root:</b> letter meanings from medieval lists — Attested lists; spread and divination — Modern."],
 "For one true question tonight, draw three fews. What are your root, trunk, and branch?"),
("lore", "Symbols", "The triskele is old; the rest is newer",
 ["Three arms turn together on stone older than any church in the parish. The triskele, the triple spiral, is cut on Iron Age metalwork of the La Tene style, the flowing art of Gaul and Britain, and on stones raised long before. It honors threes that matter: land and sea and sky, or three of your own choosing. Trace one slowly and your hand learns the turn better than your eyes ever will. Some carved stones are older still, raised long before iron was ever worked. Your hand joins a long line of hands each time you trace the turn.",
  "Leave the rest of the famous swirls where they belong. The triquetra with its three pointed loops, the shield knot, and most tangled knotwork sold as a druid badge belong to Early Christian and medieval art, the world of the Book of Kells, or to frankly modern craft. Draw them gladly as craft, and never call them druidic. One spiral here is Iron Age; the rest is newer. Loving them as craft takes nothing from them; misnaming them only clouds the water.",
  "Sketch your triskele on paper first, three arms turning the same way, in under half an hour. Try chalk on your own step next, then a scored loaf before baking. Note where each mark went and what washed or wore away, because the hand keeps what the eye only borrows. Work no larger than your palm until the turn sits in your wrist.",
  "<b>Lore Root:</b> triskele La Tene-attested — Attested; triquetra-as-druidic — cut, Modern."],
 "Before you draw your triskele, how would you describe it in words, and which way do its arms turn?"),
("journal", "Symbols", "Draw-your-triskele, page one: copy the spiral slowly. Where did your attention break?", 10),
("journal", "Symbols", "Draw-your-triskele, page two: trace what threes matter to you — land, sea, sky, or your own.", 10),
("tracker", "Symbols", "Carve-draw tracker: where did your triskele go this month?",
 ["Paper copy — date", "Doorway chalk — date", "Bread scored — date", "Stitched or drawn on cloth — date", "Garden or path marking — date", "Gift given with the mark — date", "What held, what washed away?"]),
]

CH8 = [
("lore", "Charms", "Gaulish words in Latin books",
 ["Inside a physician's handbook written in Latin around the year 400, a learned Gaul named Marcellus of Bordeaux tucked charms in his own native tongue among the prescriptions. The Gaulish words sit beside remedies for throat trouble, notes on amulets and stones, and gifts laid in wells: empire medicine with a Gaulish accent. Charms, the page proves, were spoken aloud as well as made with hands. A Gaulish voice survives inside the empire's handbook, prescribing in two tongues at once. His Gaulish survivals cluster around throat troubles, small spoken words set down amid the Latin page.",
  "Surviving Gaulish words lean toward protection and healing, and this chapter follows that line only. Three workings stand here, and every one of them wards and blesses: a knot for the road, a bag for the door, a blessing of water. Nothing here curses or hexes, because that is not the shelf this book keeps. Ward the body, bless the house, guard the road: those three verbs are the whole vocabulary here.",
  "Name one part of your life that needs guarding this week: sleep, the commute, an elder's room. Write it in a single line, leaving defeating and fixing out of the sentence. That line will feed whichever of the three workings you choose to make first. Keep the sentence where you will see it while you work, pinned above the table or first in the journal.",
  "<b>Lore Root:</b> Marcellus charms and amulets — Attested."],
 "What part of your life needs protecting now, rather than anything you wish to defeat?"),
("lore", "Charms", "Caim and lorica",
 ["Draw a ring around someone with your feet and your voice. The caim is an encircling prayer from Irish tradition: you walk sunwise around a person or a place, speaking protection as you go, until the circle closes. Beside it stands the lorica, the breastplate, a prayer that clothes the body part by part in verse, head to foot, as though dressing it in armor for the day. The turn runs sunwise, with the road of the sun, and the closing thanks seals the ring like a knot.",
  "Both shapes come down through manuscript and folk keeping, attested across prayers and custom rather than invented here. Their household sense is plain: the ring says this ground is watched, the breastplate says this body is dressed. Hallway, garden bed, and your own bedside all take the ring kindly, and your own frame takes the verses well before sleep. Walk it for another person and the meaning doubles: you lend your feet to their guarding.",
  "Walk one small caim tonight around a single room, speaking plain protection as you circle, and close it with thanks at the door. Tomorrow name your own breastplate head to foot in six short lines. One room, one body, under twenty minutes, and the shape is yours to keep. Log where you walked and what words you used; the ring grows stronger each time you close it. A week of small rings will teach you more than one grand one.",
  "<b>Lore Root:</b> caim and lorica prayers — Attested folk and manuscript tradition."],
 "In plain words, what would your own breastplate prayer name from head to foot?"),
("lore", "Charms", "First working: the knot charm",
 ["Wool or linen cord and three slow knots are the whole toolkit. Folk custom across many districts ties protection into knots, and this working keeps that shape: one line of blessing spoken over each knot as your fingers pull it snug. Give the first knot to the body, the second to the home, the third to the road, speaking each line aloud as you tie. Speak on the out-breath as the knot tightens, so voice and hands finish together. Three knots, three lines, three takings-care.",
  "Knot-tying for protection is a folk survival, gathered from many households rather than prescribed in one rite, and this monthly shape is a modern keeping built upon it. A charm left unwatched grows stale the way bread does, so the rite includes its own renewal: untie at each fire festival and tie fresh, speaking the lines anew. The retying is the devotion, not an afterthought. Date the tying on a tag or in the margin, so the festival finds you ready.",
  "Draft three lines short enough to say in one breath each, and tie your cord at the table tonight. Carry it in a pocket or hang it where trouble tends to enter: the draughty window, the bag you commute with. Twenty minutes with hands and voice, and it is done. Hang it high enough that daily brushing does not wear it thin. When the cord frays, wash it and begin again; worn fiber has simply carried its share.",
  "<b>Lore Root:</b> knot-tying across folk custom — Folk survival."],
 "Draft your three short knot lines here, keeping each one brief enough to speak aloud."),
("lore", "Charms", "Second working: the doorway bag",
 ["Above your lintel a small bag can keep the household names. Sew or tie a pouch from scrap cloth and fill it with rowan berries or a twig where rowan grows, a pinch of salt, a sprig of vervain, and a slip of paper carrying the names of those under your roof. Hang it inside the door you use most, where your eyes will find it every single day. Keep the stitching plain and the knot firm; a working bag wants honesty, not finery. A thimbleful of salt is plenty; the bag is a token, not a storehouse.",
  "Rowan at the threshold, salt at the sill, green over the door: these ingredients recur across folk custom, gathered from many households rather than one old recipe. The seasonal renewal is modern housekeeping with an old accent, fresh salt and fresh green at each fire festival, names spoken aloud as you set them back. Clearing away the old with care matters as much as hanging the new. Speak each name as you lay the paper back inside, so no one is housed unnamed.",
  "Make the bag in half an hour with needle or string and whatever your shops can supply. Carry the old salt to running water or bare soil when you renew, never the bin beside the door. List your contents and names in the journal tonight so the next renewal finds them ready. The old green goes to soil or running water with thanks, never to the bin.",
  "<b>Lore Root:</b> doorway ingredients across folk custom — Folk survival."],
 "List what your bag will hold, and write the names that will rest on its paper."),
("lore", "Charms", "Third working: the well-water blessing",
 ["Clean water in a small bottle can carry the well to your rooms. Fetch it from your own tap with thanks, or from a well where you have asked leave and taken only a little. Keep it sealed and set apart for blessings. A few drops flicked sunwise around a room, across a threshold, or upon a brow, the old pattern called aspersion, lays the water's memory in household miniature. Label the bottle and keep it high, away from drinking vessels and small hands.",
  "This working is reconstructed from well-custom rather than copied from one single rite: the asking of leave, the small taking, the sunwise turn. Its rule is thrift. Never drink from wild water, never bottle from a protected spring, never take more than the blessing needs. Change the water each month and pour what is left on the roots of a plant, so nothing is wasted and nothing harmed. Little and often is the whole method: drops, not draughts. A bottle kept a month and renewed teaches thrift better than any rule.",
  "Choose where the bottle will live and what it will bless first: the desk, the sickroom door, your own forehead before a hard day. The rite takes minutes: open, flick, thank, close. Log the first blessing with its date, and renew the water when the month turns. Bless the threshold first where you are unsure; the door you keep is hungry for it.",
  "<b>Lore Root:</b> water-aspersion pattern — Reconstructed from well-custom."],
 "Where will your blessing water live, and what will it bless first?"),
("journal", "Charms", "Charm log: which working did you make, when, and for whom? Record all three.", 12),
("journal", "Charms", "Protection log: what did you protect this week — body, home, road? Name it.", 12),
("lore", "Charms", "Uncrossing and repair",
 ["Charms fade, snag, or get overrun, and that is maintenance calling, not failure. Folk keeping expects renewal the way houses expect dusting. To uncross a working, take it apart kindly: untie the knot and wash the cord, empty the bag and sweep the sill, pour out the water and scrub the bottle. Name what slipped as you work, without shame and without hurry. Work in daylight with the windows open, and let the taking-apart feel like tidying, because it is.",
  "Remake the thing one size steadier than before: a stronger line on the knot, a better-kept round at the door, a caim walked weekly for a while instead of monthly. The renewal pattern is reconstructed from custom, and this repair-rite shape is modern, labeled so. Repair outranks perfection every time; the mending carries your care forward farther than a flawless first try. Write the repair beside the original entry, so the charm carries its history with it. Failure logged honestly beats perfection pretended.",
  "Look over your rooms tonight and find one working that needs it. Give it twenty minutes: uncross, clean, remake, log. Write what slipped in one line and what steadies it in the next. The house will feel the difference by morning. Thank the working as you close it, stale or steady; gratitude mends faster than fault-finding. Begin tonight, while the need is fresh.",
  "<b>Lore Root:</b> renewal pattern — Reconstructed; repair-rite — Modern."],
 "Look around your house. What needs uncrossing, and what is its very first step?"),
("tracker", "Charms", "Home ward tracker, week one: walk the caim, mark the day.",
 ["Day 1 — walked?", "Day 2 — walked?", "Day 3 — walked?", "Day 4 — walked?", "Day 5 — walked?", "Day 6 — walked?", "Day 7 — walked + knot re-tied?", "What held this week?"]),
("tracker", "Charms", "Home ward tracker, week two: walk the caim, mark the day.",
 ["Day 8 — walked?", "Day 9 — walked?", "Day 10 — walked?", "Day 11 — walked?", "Day 12 — walked?", "Day 13 — walked?", "Day 14 — walked + bag renewed?", "What held across both weeks?"]),
]

CH9 = [
("lore", "Ancestors", "Tech Duinn and the mounds",
 ["Far to the southwest, the old Irish tales say, lies the house of Donn, Tech Duinn, a dark island where the dead gather to rest. Closer to home, the grass-covered mounds with their stone doors were honored as kindly portals, said to stand open at Samhain so those gone before could draw near. Your people are not far off in this telling, more like neighbors toward dusk, within calling distance of the table. Say the name Tech Duinn aloud once, and feel how the syllables land like stones in deep water.",
  "Hold that nearness gently, as comfort rather than claim. The island and the open mounds come from the tales, an attested literary pattern rather than a map you could sail. Nothing here asks you to prove or perform anything. One shared meal a year, a card or two, a letter, a roll call of names: small vessels, and grief is welcome at the table with its manners intact. Come as you are, grieving or steady or numb; the table does not grade your feelings.",
  "Rest with one name tonight, with no rite required at all. Light a candle if you like, speak the name aloud, and remember one true thing about them. Write that single sentence down before sleep. When you are ready, the supper page will show you the fuller keeping. There is no test to pass and nothing to get right, only a name spoken and a sentence kept.",
  "<b>Lore Root:</b> Tech Duinn and mound-portals in the tales — Attested literary pattern."],
 "Rest a moment. Which of your dead feels nearest to you right now?"),
("lore", "Ancestors", "The dumb supper script",
 ["Lay one more plate than the living need, and keep the first half of the meal in silence. A dumb supper is a quiet supper shared with the dead: cook a full meal you can manage, set the extra place with care, light a candle for each name you will speak. Eat together in kind, companionable quiet, letting the empty chair hold its own at the table. Keep the food simple and hot: soup, bread, something from their table where you know it.",
  "Halfway through, speak each name aloud with one true sentence, thanks or memory or something left unsaid, and eat the second half carrying them with you. The shape comes down through folk custom, kept in many households in many forms, and this script is one gentle modern keeping of it. Once a year is plenty. Steady remembrance outweighs grandeur, and tears at the table are no breach of manners. Let silence do the heavy lifting; you need no speech beyond the names and one true sentence.",
  "After the meal, thank them for coming and snuff the candles. Set a portion aside for birds or neighbors in the morning. The whole keeping fits inside an hour, dishes aside. Choose your menu and your names tonight, and let the page hold them until the night arrives. Sleep with the names written beside the bed, and notice what morning brings. Keep the night unhurried; the dead are patient company.",
  "<b>Lore Root:</b> dumb-supper shape — Folk survival."],
 "What will your supper menu be, and which names do you wish to invite?"),
("journal", "Ancestors", "Ancestor card, first: name, dates, one story, one thing they left you, one question.", 13),
("journal", "Ancestors", "Ancestor card, second: name, dates, one story, one thing they left you, one question.", 13),
("journal", "Ancestors", "Grief letter plus roll-call: write the unsent letter, then list your Samhain names.", 13),
]

CH10 = [
("journal", "Oath", "Your vow page: fill it in. I will keep ___ for 30 days. I will not ___. Witnessed by ___.", 12),
("tracker", "Oath", "Thirty-day integration tracker: one line a day across realm, fire, plant, and charm.",
 ["Days 1-5: realm checked? fire kept? plant seen? charm held?", "Days 6-10: realm checked? fire kept? plant seen? charm held?", "Days 11-15: realm checked? fire kept? plant seen? charm held?", "Days 16-20: realm checked? fire kept? plant seen? charm held?", "Days 21-25: realm checked? fire kept? plant seen? charm held?", "Days 26-30: realm checked? fire kept? plant seen? charm held?", "What held all month?", "What lapsed, and what repairs it?"]),
("lore", "Oath", "Go on reading",
 ["Four old books can carry your thread past this one, and each is best met in small bites through public translations you can borrow before buying. The Tain, the Irish cattle-raid tale, opens at its ford fights. The Mabinogi, a Welsh collection, begins with Pwyll and Rhiannon in its First Branch. The Book of Invasions tells who came to Ireland wave by wave, and the great battle-tale of the gods closes the shelf with thunder. Borrow before buying, and read with a pencil: the margins of old tales reward plain questions.",
  "Meet them as stories first and lessons second, a chapter at a time with your pen nearby. These are attested primary texts in translation, not summaries, and they reward slow going. Take notes the way this book taught you: what happened, what it means at home, what small act answers it. Let each tale settle a week before you judge it. A chapter a week is a good pace; these books have waited centuries and will wait for you.",
  "Commit to one book and one starting day, and begin the next morning in the same spot with the same plain speech. An hour or less a sitting is plenty. Go in peace from these pages, and keep the door you have tended so well. Lend this book onward when you are done, and tell the next reader which page held you longest. The shelf will still be there when the thirty days are done.",
  "<b>Lore Root:</b> primary texts to read next — Attested, go meet them."],
 "Which book will you open first, and on what day will you begin?"),
]
